<Base title="About" type="profile" description="Biography of Armağan Amcalar: software architect, founder of Coyotiv, CTO of OpenServ, author of Gea Stack and cote, speaker and teacher.">
  <article>
    <header class="post-head narrow">
      <span class="meta">Biography</span>
      <h1>About</h1>
      <p class="sub">Software architect, founder, teacher. Electronics engineer by training, musician by stubbornness.</p>
    </header>
    <div class="post-cover">
      <Image src={photo} alt="Armağan Amcalar" width={1920} loading="eager" />
    </div>
    <div class="prose narrow">
      <p>
        I'm a software architect and entrepreneur based in Berlin. I founded <a href="/coyotiv/">Coyotiv</a>, a software company and engineering school, where I
        work as a fractional CTO for startups and enterprises and teach the next generation of engineers. I'm also the CTO of{' '}
        <a href="https://openserv.ai">OpenServ</a>, where we build infrastructure for AI agents, the CTO of <a href="https://neol.co">Neol</a>, and the founder
        of Dream Kid.
      </p>
      <p>
        My work has covered most of the stack, from designing integrated circuits and EEG amplifiers to multi-cloud architecture. Some of the products I led
        are used by millions of people. Most of what I've learned on the way I've tried to give back, as open source, as talks, or in a classroom.
      </p>
      <p>
        These days I spend most of my engineering time on <a href="/projects/geastack/">Gea Stack</a>, a compiler and framework that turns one TypeScript
        codebase into native applications for everything from a microcontroller to a game console.
      </p>

      <h2>Engineer</h2>
      <p>
        I studied Microelectronics Engineering at Sabancı University in Istanbul, and stayed for an MSc in Electronics Engineering on brain research. My
        undergraduate project was an EEG amplifier with dry active electrodes of my own design, which I used to map brain waves to MIDI and make music with
        them. For my thesis I built a P300 speller, a brain-computer interface that lets people type with their brain signals, which at the time was the
        fastest reported in academic literature. <a href="/academics/">The research is written up here.</a>
      </p>
      <p>
        Years later I rebuilt that system in JavaScript as <a href="/projects/brain-bits/">brain-bits</a> and took it on stage, typing “HELLO” with my brain in
        front of conference audiences across Europe.
      </p>

      <h2>Software architect</h2>
      <p>
        I've written software professionally since 2002. After leading front-end and then all of software architecture at Tart New Media in Istanbul, I
        co-founded Startup Kitchen, a venture builder, in 2012. As chief software architect I acted as interim CTO for the startups we incubated and led their
        teams.
      </p>
      <p>
        In 2015 I moved to Berlin to become Head of Software Engineering at unu, the electric scooter company, and built its engineering organisation for
        cloud, mobile, web and connected vehicles from scratch. In 2019 I joined Wayfair as a Senior Engineering Manager, leading the R&amp;D teams for supplier
        onboarding and the frontend engineering community in Berlin.
      </p>
      <p>
        I left to found Coyotiv in April 2020. The idea was to change how software is built by changing how engineers are trained, so it began as a school and grew
        into a software company and a product studio. <a href="/coyotiv/">More about Coyotiv.</a>
      </p>

      <h2>Open source</h2>
      <p>
        I published my first open source code as a teenager on Planet Source Code, the GitHub before GitHub, under the handle I still use: <a href="/projects/moving-caption/">DasherSW</a>. <a href="/projects/cote/">cote</a>, a library for zero-configuration microservices in Node.js, came out of the
        distributed systems we ran at Startup Kitchen and is still used in production around the world. <a href="/projects/gea/">Gea</a> is a compiler-first UI
        framework, and <a href="/projects/geastack/">Gea Stack</a> extends it to native devices. In between there are guitar effects in the browser, a rule
        engine, a glass shader and a few things I built only because I wanted to see whether they were possible. <a href="/projects/">All projects.</a>
      </p>

      <h2>Teacher and speaker</h2>
      <p>
        I love to teach. I was a teaching assistant for five courses at Sabancı, then ran the Tart Summer Camps from 2011 to 2013, which trained more than
        sixty new graduates into working engineers. In 2014 I founded Lonca, a voluntary, non-profit school in the spirit of the old craft guilds, which trains
        women engineers through apprenticeship. I have taught a master's course on front-end development at Sabancı, a three-volume JavaScript crash course
        with Women Techmakers Berlin, and now the full-time program at the Coyotiv School of Software Engineering.
      </p>
      <p>
        I've given {stage} talks at conferences and meetups since 2013, including JSConf Iceland, JSNation, Codemotion, iJS, JAX London, code.talks,
        DevDays Europe and TEDx, on distributed systems, software architecture, the psychology of working in technology and AI reasoning. I also run
        workshops that help first-time speakers get on stage. <a href="/talks/">Every talk, with slides and video.</a>
      </p>

      <h2>Musician</h2>
      <p>
        I live for music and the stage. I play the guitar and sing, for many years with my band Neşeli Günler Kumpanyası, in Turkey and in Germany. I also
        hosted a radio show on Metropol FM, a national station in Germany, for a year and a half. My interest in electronics never left the pedalboard: I model
        and build overdrive pedals, and more than one of my software projects exists because of a guitar.
      </p>

